1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:08,300
What does it take to turn 20 tons of steel into finely crafted automobiles in less than a day?

2
00:00:09,060 --> 00:00:15,700
Or to transform an empty foundation to a new home in just six hours?

3
00:00:16,740 --> 00:00:21,700
Every process is a one-of-a-kind journey beginning to end.

4
00:00:22,620 --> 00:00:29,540
From the fiery birth of high-tech golf clubs to the cataclysmic end of an old building.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:36,040
Get ready, because we're going from start to finish on Modern Marvel.

6
00:00:50,060 --> 00:00:56,800
The world's longest construction project, clocking in at 2,000 years, is China's Great Wall.

7
00:00:56,801 --> 00:01:02,980
It's a timeless reminder that getting from start to finish has often been a long, slow journey.

8
00:01:05,300 --> 00:01:08,780
But in today's world, slow is not an option.

9
00:01:10,360 --> 00:01:14,560
Getting from start to finish is a race, and no one wants to be left behind.

10
00:01:15,400 --> 00:01:19,040
If a manufacturer doesn't respond to that consumer pull,

11
00:01:19,380 --> 00:01:22,520
they're going to be out of business and other people are going to take their market.

12
00:01:22,521 --> 00:01:28,580
The faster you can get something to the consumer, the more likely they are to buy your product over somebody else's.

13
00:01:29,200 --> 00:01:31,700
Modern business has to put the pedal to the metal.

14
00:01:32,980 --> 00:01:33,680
The fuel?

15
00:01:34,800 --> 00:01:35,900
Incredible innovations.

16
00:01:36,860 --> 00:01:40,520
All engineered to get us from start to finish in record time.

17
00:01:42,860 --> 00:01:50,060
Using the automobile as a kickoff point, 100 years ago people just wanted some kind of better, faster transportation.

18
00:01:50,061 --> 00:01:55,300
And in 1908, Henry Ford delivered with his mass-produced Model Ts.

19
00:01:56,240 --> 00:01:59,080
Tin Lizzy's were built by the thousands on the assembly line.

20
00:02:00,220 --> 00:02:02,180
Back then, it was a revolutionary process.

21
00:02:04,900 --> 00:02:08,940
But not even Henry Ford could have predicted how things would change.

22
00:02:12,520 --> 00:02:15,740
Kia Motors Manufacturing in West Point, Georgia

23
00:02:15,741 --> 00:02:24,520
is a $1 billion, 650-acre arena of high tech.

24
00:02:25,740 --> 00:02:27,820
Everything in this state-of-the-art facility

25
00:02:27,821 --> 00:02:31,800
is precisely calibrated for maximum efficiency and speed.

26
00:02:34,240 --> 00:02:37,940
Each vehicle will be built in 23.3 hours,

27
00:02:38,360 --> 00:02:39,800
and there's no room for error.

28
00:02:40,380 --> 00:02:43,880
When we have 100,000 miles of warranty and 10 years of warranty,

29
00:02:43,881 --> 00:02:47,500
You want everything to be perfect when it comes off the final line.

30
00:02:48,280 --> 00:02:50,720
And the clock starts ticking here.

31
00:02:51,400 --> 00:02:53,760
This is the absolute beginning of a car.

32
00:02:54,240 --> 00:02:59,060
You wouldn't imagine it, but very soon, once it goes through our presses,

33
00:02:59,560 --> 00:03:01,680
it'll start looking like a vehicle component.

34
00:03:02,480 --> 00:03:06,660
Each one of these galvanized steel coils weighs 44,000 pounds.

35
00:03:07,340 --> 00:03:09,800
When unrolled, they stretch for more than a mile.

36
00:03:09,801 --> 00:03:21,420
The coils are lifted by 50-ton overhead cranes and placed on an enormous spool, which feeds the raw steel through a blanking press.

37
00:03:23,100 --> 00:03:26,493
This massive machine has a simple job.

38
00:03:26,494 --> 00:03:30,780
Unroll the steel coil, then cut it down to size.

39
00:03:30,781 --> 00:03:33,960
These coils will make fenders, doors,

40
00:03:33,961 --> 00:03:36,480
enters and outers, hood enters and outers,

41
00:03:36,481 --> 00:03:39,580
tailgate enters and outers, and the other parts

42
00:03:39,581 --> 00:03:41,500
that you see forming of the body.

43
00:03:41,501 --> 00:03:44,060
With its fast moving dyes, the blanking press

44
00:03:44,061 --> 00:03:47,100
can cut up to 40 panels each minute.

45
00:03:47,101 --> 00:03:50,700
You can see we have a blank storage area nearby

46
00:03:50,701 --> 00:03:52,960
where all of those panels will be stored

47
00:03:52,961 --> 00:03:57,300
based on what part they'll be stamped into.

48
00:03:57,301 --> 00:03:59,580
The panels are then fed through the stamping press,

49
00:03:59,581 --> 00:04:01,700
the largest machine in the entire factory.

50
00:04:02,560 --> 00:04:05,580
This is absolutely the biggest machine in this whole plant.

51
00:04:06,240 --> 00:04:06,980
Yes, it is.

52
00:04:07,460 --> 00:04:08,460
It's a monster.

53
00:04:09,560 --> 00:04:12,060
It weighs in at more than 5,000 tons.

54
00:04:13,320 --> 00:04:16,340
Its massive dies move at 15 strokes a minute,

55
00:04:17,260 --> 00:04:19,160
molding the tough steel as if it were clay.

56
00:04:20,060 --> 00:04:23,260
The draw die is 2,400 tons of pressure.

57
00:04:23,840 --> 00:04:27,400
Then the three remaining dies are each 1,000 tons of pressure.

58
00:04:27,401 --> 00:04:30,800
So that's a lot of force coming down.

59
00:04:30,801 --> 00:04:39,900
After less than an hour in the stamping shop, what was once an enormous steel coil is now

60
00:04:39,901 --> 00:04:43,080
a stack of shiny new auto body parts.

61
00:04:43,081 --> 00:04:46,500
But we're just getting started.

62
00:04:46,501 --> 00:04:51,740
Next up is one of the most automated departments in the entire automotive industry.

63
00:04:51,741 --> 00:04:54,340
Key is welding shop.

64
00:04:54,341 --> 00:04:59,520
there are just 62 humans and 242 robots.

65
00:05:00,120 --> 00:05:03,140
The robots improve your manufacturing abilities greatly

66
00:05:03,141 --> 00:05:05,520
because the repeatability is outstanding.

67
00:05:05,940 --> 00:05:07,580
So the same process every time,

68
00:05:07,860 --> 00:05:08,980
takes out the human error,

69
00:05:09,120 --> 00:05:11,180
so when the customer gets a good quality vehicle.

70
00:05:11,860 --> 00:05:13,880
Each vehicle that goes through the welding shop

71
00:05:13,881 --> 00:05:16,200
will receive more than 2,000 welds,

72
00:05:16,460 --> 00:05:19,700
all performed flawlessly by robots.

73
00:05:20,300 --> 00:05:21,540
The older assembly plant,

74
00:05:21,541 --> 00:05:24,380
A lot of this welding function was done using a manual gun.

75
00:05:24,960 --> 00:05:28,020
Human operators could take an entire day to complete a car body.

76
00:05:28,720 --> 00:05:31,320
Robots can fuse one together in 90 minutes.

77
00:05:34,820 --> 00:05:40,360
It was 1961 when the first industrial robots went to work for a General Motors plant.

78
00:05:41,680 --> 00:05:46,880
These robots, called Unimate, were basically mechanized lifters and welders.

79
00:05:46,881 --> 00:05:52,580
They were cutting edge for the time, but they still had plenty of drawbacks.

80
00:05:53,760 --> 00:05:56,860
They were backed up by very limited computer power.

81
00:05:57,860 --> 00:06:01,580
They weren't really equipped with any kind of sensing.

82
00:06:01,980 --> 00:06:03,880
They couldn't touch anything and feel.

83
00:06:04,240 --> 00:06:07,120
They couldn't see anything and feed it back into their system.

84
00:06:07,600 --> 00:06:12,360
So as good as they were, they were still relatively unintelligent.

85
00:06:12,361 --> 00:06:15,800
But as the decades passed and technology took off,

86
00:06:16,560 --> 00:06:19,660
robots with sensors and computer vision became commonplace.

87
00:06:21,480 --> 00:06:26,020
Today at Kia, sophisticated robotic systems perform the most difficult,

88
00:06:26,920 --> 00:06:29,800
dangerous, and repetitive tasks.

89
00:06:31,520 --> 00:06:33,520
From start to finish.

90
00:06:34,100 --> 00:06:35,960
This is the beginning. This is our floor line here.

91
00:06:36,420 --> 00:06:39,040
This is where our front aprons and dash floor comes together.

92
00:06:39,041 --> 00:06:40,920
You can see they transfer down the line here.

93
00:06:40,921 --> 00:06:43,480
and when they transfer, the robots come in and make whales.

94
00:06:44,080 --> 00:06:46,980
The jig turns back around, comes back up, robot picks it up,

95
00:06:47,060 --> 00:06:49,400
puts it back up into a carrier that goes through the overhead.

96
00:06:49,800 --> 00:06:53,920
The overhead conveyor transports the floor of the car to the body build line,

97
00:06:54,380 --> 00:06:56,140
where the vehicle starts to take shape.

98
00:06:57,020 --> 00:06:59,240
Most people think the body shop's the place to be.

99
00:06:59,320 --> 00:07:00,320
You get all the automation.

100
00:07:00,900 --> 00:07:03,720
Everything's working so fast, and when you watch the whole system run,

101
00:07:03,760 --> 00:07:04,580
it's really impressive.

102
00:07:05,480 --> 00:07:09,440
With their free range of movement and precision programming,

103
00:07:09,441 --> 00:07:16,440
Industrial robots almost look like they have minds of their own, but they don't.

104
00:07:16,441 --> 00:07:20,440
Each robot is controlled by an individual robot controller.

105
00:07:20,441 --> 00:07:23,440
It controls all the motor functions, positioning data.

106
00:07:23,441 --> 00:07:27,440
Every robot has got to be interlocked or you have collisions all over the place.

107
00:07:27,441 --> 00:07:30,440
You've got your human-machine interface right here,

108
00:07:30,441 --> 00:07:33,440
and it allows the robots to quickly communicate to each other

109
00:07:33,441 --> 00:07:36,440
to prevent collisions and unnecessary downtime.

110
00:07:36,441 --> 00:07:42,620
This interface is critical, especially here at Roof Assembly, where precisely choreographed

111
00:07:42,621 --> 00:07:45,320
machines have one minute to do their job.

112
00:07:45,321 --> 00:07:51,780
We've got to run a car every 60 seconds, so the robot's got to go in, do its job, and

113
00:07:51,781 --> 00:07:58,440
get out of the way in that 60 seconds.

114
00:07:58,441 --> 00:08:03,660
One small thing that we should realize with robotic systems is that once it's programmed

115
00:08:03,661 --> 00:08:07,940
correctly, that robot is not going to get tired.

116
00:08:07,941 --> 00:08:11,620
It's not going to make a mistake.

117
00:08:11,621 --> 00:08:14,160
After just an hour and a half, what

118
00:08:14,161 --> 00:08:18,660
was once individual doors, vendors, roofs, and floors

119
00:08:18,661 --> 00:08:24,980
are now recognizable vehicle shells in need of a paint job.

120
00:08:24,981 --> 00:08:27,880
While robots have revolutionized how the automotive industry

121
00:08:27,881 --> 00:08:33,660
gets from start to finish, they've also scored a hole in one.

122
00:08:33,661 --> 00:08:43,900
For the golf club industry, meet Ping Man, a robotic golfer designed and used exclusively by Ping Incorporated in Phoenix, Arizona.

123
00:08:44,340 --> 00:08:46,400
He's designed to mimic a human swing.

124
00:08:46,760 --> 00:08:52,280
So you can see he's got a hip bend here, a shoulder turn, which is where this whole swing is powered from.

125
00:08:52,480 --> 00:08:54,000
And then you can see it's a free wrist.

126
00:08:55,340 --> 00:08:59,260
Ping Man is the one engineers turn to as they tee up a new design.

127
00:08:59,261 --> 00:09:05,160
It's the first step in creating a golf club, and it all starts with gathering data.

128
00:09:05,880 --> 00:09:13,451
You can see exactly what golf club's spinning the ball more, which golf club is more prone to slice it, which one's more prone to hook it, which one's going to launch

129
00:09:13,452 --> 00:09:15,640
it higher, which one's going to launch it lower.

130
00:09:19,440 --> 00:09:26,540
Designing a club is serious business, and it's attempted only by the finest engineering minds around.

131
00:09:26,541 --> 00:09:28,888
It wasn't always this way.

132
00:09:28,889 --> 00:09:34,940
In the early days of golf, clubs were basic, just a hunk of wood or

133
00:09:34,941 --> 00:09:36,654
metal attached to a stick.

134
00:09:36,655 --> 00:09:40,940
But the late Karsten Solheim, Ping's founder, changed all of that

135
00:09:40,941 --> 00:09:47,114
forever. Karsten wasn't a good golfer, but he was a brilliant engineer.

136
00:09:47,115 --> 00:09:48,940
My dad was phenomenal

137
00:09:48,941 --> 00:09:49,720
mechanically.

138
00:09:49,721 --> 00:09:54,880
And I mean, before General Electric, where he designed their first compact television,

139
00:09:54,881 --> 00:09:59,080
He worked for Ryan Aircraft with the engineers that did Lindbergh's plane.

140
00:09:59,540 --> 00:10:07,060
He started to play golf and thought, you know what, I could apply some general engineering principles to these golf clubs.

141
00:10:07,620 --> 00:10:12,020
In 1959, Karsten set out to make a putter unlike any in existence.

142
00:10:13,220 --> 00:10:19,060
He spent months painstakingly designing, handcrafted, and fine-tuning his first putter.

143
00:10:20,060 --> 00:10:24,660
His diligence paid off when the new putter actually lowered his golf score.

144
00:10:24,880 --> 00:10:28,940
What he didn't realize he was building at the same time was a tuning fork.

145
00:10:29,660 --> 00:10:35,520
So when he hit the first ball, it went ping, and he knew the name of his putter.

146
00:10:36,120 --> 00:10:37,600
Fast forward half a century.

147
00:10:38,040 --> 00:10:42,440
Fierce competition in the golf world means spending months on a design is not an option.

148
00:10:44,900 --> 00:10:48,700
Today, Ping's engineers can whip up a design in about half a day.

149
00:10:50,420 --> 00:10:51,520
Here's how they do it.

150
00:10:51,521 --> 00:10:56,920
Data from PingMan goes into a computerized database.

151
00:10:56,921 --> 00:11:00,540
Engineers quickly pull information and design a new club

152
00:11:00,541 --> 00:11:04,520
in cyberspace. Then

153
00:11:04,521 --> 00:11:08,240
they test it. So what we're modeling here is a collision between a golf club and a

154
00:11:08,241 --> 00:11:08,960
golf ball.

155
00:11:08,961 --> 00:11:12,680
We're looking at the stresses and the bending and we just want to make sure

156
00:11:12,681 --> 00:11:14,720
that nothing's taking on too much stress or bending

157
00:11:14,721 --> 00:11:18,500
and that it's gonna have a good solid feel for the customer. With sophisticated

158
00:11:18,501 --> 00:11:21,160
computer programs and just a few clicks of the mouse,

159
00:11:21,161 --> 00:11:26,060
this virtual golf club is calibrated and ready to swing its way into existence.

160
00:11:27,640 --> 00:11:30,980
You know, they say it's not rocket science, but here it actually kind of is.

161
00:11:31,980 --> 00:11:36,120
The design is finished, but there's a lot more to do before you can tee off,

162
00:11:37,880 --> 00:11:39,760
including melting steel.

163
00:11:40,700 --> 00:11:47,800
As for those newly minted cars, next stop is a dip in an 86,000-gallon electrified tank.

164
00:11:47,801 --> 00:11:52,140
And it's start to finish in reverse.

165
00:11:52,960 --> 00:11:55,140
Next on Modern Marvels.

166
00:11:55,820 --> 00:11:58,540
Start to finish the Great Wall of China.

167
00:11:59,760 --> 00:12:02,180
Construction began in the 7th century B.C.

168
00:12:02,181 --> 00:12:04,940
and continued until the 16th century A.D.

169
00:12:04,941 --> 00:12:07,980
The wall stretches for more than 4,000 miles

170
00:12:07,981 --> 00:12:10,360
and is among the world's greatest wonders.

171
00:12:14,420 --> 00:12:16,700
From automated construction workers,

172
00:12:16,701 --> 00:12:19,780
to robotic researchers,

173
00:12:20,880 --> 00:12:23,240
getting from start to finish has never been faster.

174
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:26,160
Mega machines slice,

175
00:12:28,100 --> 00:12:28,800
mold,

176
00:12:29,960 --> 00:12:31,680
and fuse their way to a car body

177
00:12:31,681 --> 00:12:34,020
in just two hours.

178
00:12:35,920 --> 00:12:39,200
And designers cook up an amazingly complex golf club

179
00:12:39,201 --> 00:12:41,560
faster than you can say sand trap.

180
00:12:42,820 --> 00:12:44,200
But in some worlds,

181
00:12:44,201 --> 00:12:47,580
crossing the finish line doesn't mean you get a shiny new creation.

182
00:12:49,040 --> 00:12:51,640
Sometimes it leaves you with a pile of rubble.

183
00:12:52,880 --> 00:12:54,920
And that's just what these guys want.

184
00:12:55,380 --> 00:12:56,980
Because they're demolition men.

185
00:12:57,720 --> 00:12:59,520
And their job isn't to create.

186
00:13:00,180 --> 00:13:01,380
It's to destroy.

187
00:13:04,380 --> 00:13:05,920
Downtown Hartford, Connecticut.

188
00:13:06,920 --> 00:13:10,280
A busy urban area with shiny high-rises.

189
00:13:10,660 --> 00:13:12,000
Modern architecture.

190
00:13:12,640 --> 00:13:13,620
And this.

191
00:13:14,200 --> 00:13:18,360
an 80,000 square foot eyesore

192
00:13:18,361 --> 00:13:20,940
that once housed a news channel.

193
00:13:22,160 --> 00:13:23,620
The building has to go,

194
00:13:24,620 --> 00:13:26,360
but that won't be easy.

195
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:29,940
This building is built in the 60s.

196
00:13:30,620 --> 00:13:32,760
They built it like a fortress, you know?

197
00:13:33,120 --> 00:13:35,280
It's just amazing how they built this structure

198
00:13:35,281 --> 00:13:36,200
with so much steel.

199
00:13:36,760 --> 00:13:38,280
The industrial wrecking company

200
00:13:38,281 --> 00:13:40,900
will have five months to complete this project.

201
00:13:40,901 --> 00:13:45,040
It's a serious schedule.

202
00:13:46,580 --> 00:13:49,660
And if it's not met, it'll cost a pretty penny.

203
00:13:51,520 --> 00:13:53,880
You know, depending on the size of the project,

204
00:13:54,160 --> 00:13:56,740
it could be a lot of money, a couple of thousand dollars a day.

205
00:13:57,220 --> 00:13:58,740
The battle lines have been drawn.

206
00:13:59,580 --> 00:14:01,360
It'll be a five-month showdown.

207
00:14:01,940 --> 00:14:05,500
Man and machine versus a tough concrete and steel building.

208
00:14:06,060 --> 00:14:07,420
Who will come out on top?

209
00:14:10,900 --> 00:14:19,580
Unusual things happen.

210
00:14:19,700 --> 00:14:21,660
It depends on how well a building was built,

211
00:14:21,760 --> 00:14:24,960
and unfortunately, even if you have the plans

212
00:14:24,961 --> 00:14:26,500
of how a building was built,

213
00:14:26,640 --> 00:14:28,620
it doesn't tell you how strong the building is.

214
00:14:29,560 --> 00:14:32,240
Three months in, and the project has been running smoothly.

215
00:14:35,540 --> 00:14:36,480
Until now.

216
00:14:36,481 --> 00:14:43,480
Construction engineers are quickly realizing the building is much stronger than they thought.

217
00:14:43,481 --> 00:14:50,480
To stay on schedule, engineers have to create a significant structural failure.

218
00:14:50,481 --> 00:14:56,480
They zero in on two columns holding up the upper level.

219
00:14:56,481 --> 00:15:03,480
By attaching choker cables to the beams and using an excavator,

220
00:15:03,481 --> 00:15:06,480
and using an excavator.

221
00:15:06,481 --> 00:15:08,900
Engineers hope they'll make quick work of a large part

222
00:15:08,901 --> 00:15:11,140
of the building.

223
00:15:11,141 --> 00:15:14,440
The excavator is a Cat 245.

224
00:15:14,441 --> 00:15:18,360
It weighs in at 140,000 pounds.

225
00:15:18,361 --> 00:15:21,540
It's usually a demo man's best friend.

226
00:15:21,541 --> 00:15:23,800
But today, this monster of destruction

227
00:15:23,801 --> 00:15:24,860
is having a rough go.

228
00:15:28,500 --> 00:15:32,300
What was unexpected was when we pulled out the second one,

229
00:15:32,301 --> 00:15:33,400
The building didn't come down.

230
00:15:34,620 --> 00:15:36,580
It just settled a little bit.

231
00:15:38,180 --> 00:15:45,180
That's a attribution to the well-built concrete construction that they used back in the early 60s.

232
00:15:45,720 --> 00:15:48,540
The wrecking crew doesn't have time to fight with this building.

233
00:15:49,140 --> 00:15:50,940
They know they need more ammo.

234
00:15:52,360 --> 00:15:54,100
Enter the pulverizer.

235
00:15:55,300 --> 00:15:59,960
With up to 90 tons of crushing force, the pulverizer is a beast.

236
00:16:00,960 --> 00:16:03,620
Its jaws open up six to eight feet across,

237
00:16:04,420 --> 00:16:06,980
and it chews through concrete as if it were cardboard.

238
00:16:10,080 --> 00:16:13,460
2.45, munched into the floor a little bit to loosen it up.

239
00:16:13,461 --> 00:16:27,461
And then he reached up with his big boom and pushed down on the top of the fourth floor

240
00:16:32,280 --> 00:16:34,980
to push the building down into the hall.

241
00:16:36,980 --> 00:16:39,940
Once he got it going, then the weight took it the rest of the way down.

242
00:16:43,460 --> 00:16:50,820
Tons of concrete and steel that defied the elements for more than 50 years, demolished

243
00:16:50,821 --> 00:16:53,820
in mere seconds.

244
00:16:53,821 --> 00:17:04,000
And with that, this job just made considerable progress.

245
00:17:04,001 --> 00:17:06,940
But there's still a long way to go before the building falls.

246
00:17:06,941 --> 00:17:09,880
It's definitely built like a bomb shelter.

247
00:17:09,881 --> 00:17:11,700
We didn't anticipate it being this.

248
00:17:11,701 --> 00:17:14,020
Now, the rebar is all over the place.

249
00:17:14,440 --> 00:17:16,080
As you can see, it's spaghetti up there.

250
00:17:17,140 --> 00:17:19,040
Probably about 8 to 10-inch floors.

251
00:17:20,780 --> 00:17:25,080
The excavator, even with a pulverizer, can't do this job on its own.

252
00:17:25,720 --> 00:17:26,900
It's taking too long.

253
00:17:27,480 --> 00:17:30,100
And the last thing this wrecking crew has is time.

254
00:17:30,760 --> 00:17:33,020
So now they call in the big guns.

255
00:17:34,100 --> 00:17:35,300
The wrecking ball.

256
00:17:35,860 --> 00:17:40,900
There are a lot of restrictions with regard to wrecking ball in built-up surroundings like in cities.

257
00:17:40,901 --> 00:17:45,480
Because the contractor was having a difficult time with some of the concrete in certain areas,

258
00:17:45,580 --> 00:17:48,040
and he had to use it vertically, not swinging it enough.

259
00:17:49,140 --> 00:17:54,780
In other words, this massive three-and-a-half-ton chunk of metal will be dropped on top of the building,

260
00:17:55,000 --> 00:17:58,160
using gravity to ram through the thick layers of concrete.

261
00:17:58,980 --> 00:18:03,200
You pretty much strategically hit the building, so gravity will do most of your demolition.

262
00:18:04,140 --> 00:18:07,120
Sounds easy enough, but it's not.

263
00:18:07,121 --> 00:18:19,000
Controlling a 7,000 pound chunk of metal, dangling from the end of an 80 foot boom, is extremely dangerous.

264
00:18:22,160 --> 00:18:23,695
Al Capaziello would know.

265
00:18:23,696 --> 00:18:27,380
He spent more than 60 years working with the ball and crane.

266
00:18:28,900 --> 00:18:34,140
The wrecking ball is like a dinosaur. It's also like a boomerang.

267
00:18:34,141 --> 00:18:38,240
like it's continuously turning, turning, spinning.

268
00:18:38,980 --> 00:18:41,440
And it goes out and it comes back at you.

269
00:18:42,360 --> 00:18:45,320
It can't take you out of that ball for a second.

270
00:18:46,320 --> 00:18:48,360
It will come right in the cab and kill you.

271
00:18:49,360 --> 00:18:52,660
Al managed to survive decades working with the old ball.

272
00:18:53,420 --> 00:18:56,080
Today, he's keeping a watchful eye on his brother Craig,

273
00:18:56,880 --> 00:18:58,020
who's running the crane.

274
00:18:58,021 --> 00:19:08,260
Right now we're, we've got some little ball action going on, bringing the top down funds,

275
00:19:08,261 --> 00:19:10,420
brings us back to the old days.

276
00:19:10,421 --> 00:19:15,100
These days we're getting a little more modernized in the industry with high reach excavators

277
00:19:15,101 --> 00:19:17,460
and things like that.

278
00:19:17,461 --> 00:19:21,080
The old ball harkens back to days of yore.

279
00:19:21,081 --> 00:19:26,480
The wrecking ball is certainly not the most technologically advanced instrument of destruction,

280
00:19:26,481 --> 00:19:28,720
But it works.

281
00:19:28,721 --> 00:19:33,200
And since its birth in early 20th century New York City,

282
00:19:33,201 --> 00:19:37,200
it has rammed its way through building after building.

283
00:19:40,240 --> 00:19:43,520
And on this job, the crew used it wisely.

284
00:19:43,521 --> 00:19:46,660
No accidents, no slowdowns.

285
00:19:49,700 --> 00:19:51,920
A good crew on the ground is a critical part

286
00:19:51,921 --> 00:19:54,540
of a successful start to finish.

287
00:19:54,541 --> 00:19:55,920
Terrence Hegel knows this.

288
00:19:56,180 --> 00:19:57,660
He's in the building industry,

289
00:19:57,920 --> 00:20:01,680
and today he's assembled a top-notch crew to complete a big job,

290
00:20:02,360 --> 00:20:08,900
going from this to this in just six hours.

291
00:20:09,580 --> 00:20:12,780
Final house will be what we call traditional seashore colonial,

292
00:20:13,240 --> 00:20:16,180
so have a little wraparound front porch, lots of windows,

293
00:20:16,580 --> 00:20:19,740
two stories, three bedrooms, two-and-a-half baths.

294
00:20:19,741 --> 00:20:22,580
I have a full walk-up attic. It's a very nice house.

295
00:20:22,581 --> 00:20:24,600
But it's no ordinary house.

296
00:20:25,380 --> 00:20:27,600
This is a custom-designed modular home

297
00:20:27,601 --> 00:20:30,340
where all the components are fabricated in a factory

298
00:20:30,341 --> 00:20:32,960
and designed to snap together like Legos.

299
00:20:33,520 --> 00:20:35,280
The part that I most enjoy

300
00:20:35,281 --> 00:20:37,900
is starting at 7 o'clock in the morning

301
00:20:37,901 --> 00:20:39,620
and seeing that empty foundation

302
00:20:39,621 --> 00:20:43,480
and watching my guys come in and get ready to go to work.

303
00:20:44,580 --> 00:20:46,260
At the end of that workday,

304
00:20:46,580 --> 00:20:48,140
which might be six or eight hours,

305
00:20:48,440 --> 00:20:49,560
we're going to have a house

306
00:20:49,561 --> 00:20:52,280
that would have taken anybody else three to four months.

307
00:20:52,580 --> 00:20:58,580
What we have here is our first module that we'll set today.

308
00:20:58,581 --> 00:21:03,080
About seven days ago, this house was just a pile of lumber, and you can see right here

309
00:21:03,081 --> 00:21:04,760
it's nearly a completed house.

310
00:21:04,761 --> 00:21:07,260
The full kitchen is in.

311
00:21:07,261 --> 00:21:14,000
These modules can weigh more than 25,000 pounds, making the crane a key player on this job.

312
00:21:14,001 --> 00:21:18,640
With the crane, you really want an experienced operator, and Ronnie is a very experienced

313
00:21:18,641 --> 00:21:19,640
operator.

314
00:21:19,641 --> 00:21:20,900
We've been working with him for years.

315
00:21:20,901 --> 00:21:21,900
It's a dangerous job.

316
00:21:21,901 --> 00:21:23,380
He's got to worry about power lines.

317
00:21:23,460 --> 00:21:24,740
He has to worry about the wind.

318
00:21:25,080 --> 00:21:28,180
He's got to worry about someone not paying attention and wandering around.

319
00:21:28,340 --> 00:21:30,340
So the operator is critical.

320
00:21:31,380 --> 00:21:36,840
In fact, this entire crew is highly specialized, proof that modular is a hot market.

321
00:21:38,180 --> 00:21:41,440
Typically, when you build a house, you have a construction loan, okay?

322
00:21:41,860 --> 00:21:47,300
And your construction loan starts ticking the minute you start construction and start borrowing money from the bank.

323
00:21:47,301 --> 00:21:54,140
If it takes six months to build a typical house and I can finish that same house in two months,

324
00:21:54,380 --> 00:21:58,000
that's four months of interest that that particular homeowner's not paying.

325
00:21:58,140 --> 00:22:00,820
That's a significant dollar savings to the homeowner.

326
00:22:01,480 --> 00:22:04,720
But assembling cubes on site is just one part of this process.

327
00:22:05,280 --> 00:22:08,520
The nuts and bolts of modular building is in the factory.

328
00:22:09,800 --> 00:22:12,780
What I find amazing is the factory process.

329
00:22:13,040 --> 00:22:16,220
And you have to see it to believe it.

330
00:22:16,221 --> 00:22:24,700
At a modular home factory, it's always go time, as lumber becomes living quarters in an industrial blink of an eye.

331
00:22:26,960 --> 00:22:30,940
Plus, fire and metal combine to give birth to a golf club.

332
00:22:31,440 --> 00:22:35,400
And a shocking bath is the next step for these bathing beauties.

333
00:22:36,040 --> 00:22:38,180
That's next on Modern Marvels.

334
00:22:39,360 --> 00:22:42,980
Start to finish, the demolition of Seattle's kingdom.

335
00:22:42,981 --> 00:22:43,681
Dome.

336
00:22:43,682 --> 00:22:51,300
Bringing down the dome's 25,000-ton roof took more than 4,000 pounds of dynamite and 21.6

337
00:22:51,301 --> 00:22:53,553
miles of detonation cord.

338
00:22:53,554 --> 00:22:59,500
Back at Kia Motors, it's been just two and a half hours, and these

339
00:22:59,501 --> 00:23:05,734
20-ton coils of steel are close to becoming new sets of wheels.

340
00:23:05,735 --> 00:23:08,900
But there's still some traveling

341
00:23:08,901 --> 00:23:10,240
to do.

342
00:23:10,241 --> 00:23:15,460
And the most precarious pit stop is just ahead, the paint shop.

343
00:23:15,461 --> 00:23:18,800
Each new car will spend about one third of its early life

344
00:23:18,801 --> 00:23:20,400
in paint.

345
00:23:20,401 --> 00:23:21,900
The whole process from this point

346
00:23:21,901 --> 00:23:23,460
is a little over eight hours.

347
00:23:23,461 --> 00:23:27,080
There's roughly six miles of conveyor in this plant.

348
00:23:27,081 --> 00:23:29,560
So it takes a while to travel that.

349
00:23:29,561 --> 00:23:31,360
It's way too much of a time investment

350
00:23:31,361 --> 00:23:33,780
to risk even the smallest error.

351
00:23:33,781 --> 00:23:37,460
So the plant takes extreme precautions here.

352
00:23:37,461 --> 00:23:41,460
This shop is as clean as the most sterile operating room.

353
00:23:41,461 --> 00:23:45,160
Everything that enters must be thoroughly decontaminated.

354
00:23:45,161 --> 00:23:50,500
That goes for humans, as well as cars,

355
00:23:50,501 --> 00:23:52,120
because the slightest speck of dust

356
00:23:52,121 --> 00:23:53,760
can ruin a shiny new paint job.

357
00:23:57,460 --> 00:24:01,440
The first step is pretreatment, a series of washes

358
00:24:01,441 --> 00:24:04,140
and chemical dips that prep the vehicle surface for paint.

359
00:24:04,141 --> 00:24:13,280
It's a laborious process, but the payoff is an immaculate metal canvas ready for customized

360
00:24:13,281 --> 00:24:14,560
color.

361
00:24:14,561 --> 00:24:17,060
And that first coat of paint packs quite a punch.

362
00:24:17,061 --> 00:24:19,700
The proper term is electro deposition.

363
00:24:19,701 --> 00:24:22,700
We dip the unit and take a electrically charged paint.

364
00:24:22,701 --> 00:24:27,900
It coats not only the outer surfaces, but the inner cavities and crevices and the underside

365
00:24:27,901 --> 00:24:30,020
of the unit.

366
00:24:30,021 --> 00:24:37,500
This 86,000 gallon bath is about 90% deionized water and 10% paint solids.

367
00:24:37,501 --> 00:24:40,060
And did we mention it's supercharged with electricity?

368
00:24:40,061 --> 00:24:42,620
This is a DC current.

369
00:24:42,621 --> 00:24:44,200
It's very high voltage.

370
00:24:44,201 --> 00:24:47,640
We cannot let anybody in there while it's turned on.

371
00:24:47,641 --> 00:24:52,920
The cars are grounded with a copper strip on the conveyor.

372
00:24:52,921 --> 00:24:57,320
And because opposite electrical charges attract, the paint particles bind themselves to the

373
00:24:57,321 --> 00:25:00,660
the car is as if the metal were a magnet.

374
00:25:00,661 --> 00:25:02,080
It's corrosion prevention.

375
00:25:02,081 --> 00:25:04,440
It coats the inside and the outside of the car.

376
00:25:04,441 --> 00:25:06,980
So it's a rust preventative, or body seams,

377
00:25:06,981 --> 00:25:09,220
and things that you can't see, and the things you really

378
00:25:09,221 --> 00:25:11,480
can't get to with paint.

379
00:25:11,481 --> 00:25:13,320
It takes six minutes for each shell

380
00:25:13,321 --> 00:25:17,020
to pass through the electro-deposition tank.

381
00:25:17,021 --> 00:25:18,940
And once they're coated, these cars

382
00:25:18,941 --> 00:25:21,880
are practically rust-proof.

383
00:25:21,881 --> 00:25:24,300
Next stop, the paint booth.

384
00:25:24,301 --> 00:25:27,300
This is where all the prepping pays off.

385
00:25:27,301 --> 00:25:31,460
as cars get the one coat the whole world will see.

386
00:25:31,461 --> 00:25:33,560
Here they take no chances.

387
00:25:33,561 --> 00:25:36,580
Everything in the paint booth is controlled by computers

388
00:25:36,581 --> 00:25:39,020
and performed by robots.

389
00:25:39,021 --> 00:25:42,700
Inside the robot arm, there is what we call a color valve.

390
00:25:42,701 --> 00:25:44,800
We paint currently 10 different colors,

391
00:25:44,801 --> 00:25:48,580
and all 10 colors of paint are piped up to that color valve.

392
00:25:48,581 --> 00:25:52,140
As the unit comes into the robot's work envelope,

393
00:25:52,141 --> 00:25:53,780
it knows what color it's going to spray.

394
00:25:53,781 --> 00:25:56,060
So it triggers the color valve, and it

395
00:25:56,061 --> 00:25:59,900
sprays the color required for that unit.

396
00:25:59,901 --> 00:26:03,700
Each car has already been outfitted with an ID number.

397
00:26:03,701 --> 00:26:05,460
Computer tracking throughout the factory

398
00:26:05,461 --> 00:26:08,720
guarantees that every car gets its required color.

399
00:26:08,721 --> 00:26:12,500
We can paint a white car, a red car, a green car in that order.

400
00:26:12,501 --> 00:26:14,800
And all we have to do is a quick purge and cleaning

401
00:26:14,801 --> 00:26:17,260
of the line that goes out the end of the robot.

402
00:26:17,261 --> 00:26:20,540
And then we load a new color in behind it.

403
00:26:20,541 --> 00:26:22,340
Flat gray is out.

404
00:26:22,341 --> 00:26:25,180
Color is in.

405
00:26:25,181 --> 00:26:29,500
After eight hours in the paint shop, we're one step closer to hitting the highway.

406
00:26:31,040 --> 00:26:35,580
From start to finish, the paint shop is engineered to prevent costly mistakes.

407
00:26:36,940 --> 00:26:41,500
And because of the incredible automation, it can almost run itself.

408
00:26:43,880 --> 00:26:46,640
Perhaps this is a glimpse into the factory of the future.

409
00:26:46,900 --> 00:26:52,300
The more sophisticated our robotic systems and machine tools become,

410
00:26:52,301 --> 00:26:56,220
We're just going slowly but surely towards the fully automated factory.

411
00:26:57,340 --> 00:27:00,920
Extreme automation plays a crucial role when it comes to making a car.

412
00:27:01,680 --> 00:27:07,900
But when it's time to forge a cutting-edge golf club, automation takes a backseat to ancient artistry.

413
00:27:10,620 --> 00:27:14,160
It's called the lost wax process. It dates back 5,000 years.

414
00:27:15,060 --> 00:27:18,100
Pete Polian runs the foundry that makes Ping golf clubs.

415
00:27:18,560 --> 00:27:21,380
He specializes in the lost wax process.

416
00:27:22,300 --> 00:27:24,560
one of the oldest methods of casting.

417
00:27:26,800 --> 00:27:29,680
And the next step in the birth of a golf club.

418
00:27:31,040 --> 00:27:35,520
It's an intense four-day process, start to finish.

419
00:27:36,920 --> 00:27:39,600
Pink golf clubs are made out of 17.4 pH.

420
00:27:40,000 --> 00:27:42,520
It's a stainless steel. It's precipitation hardening.

421
00:27:42,960 --> 00:27:45,940
It's got about 15.5 to 16% chrome.

422
00:27:46,320 --> 00:27:50,800
There's about 3% copper and about 4 to 4.5% nickel in this.

423
00:27:50,801 --> 00:27:57,800
Into the fire they go. The furnace is blazing at 3,000 degrees Fahrenheit.

424
00:27:57,801 --> 00:28:01,800
It's so hot, the steel will take less than an hour to melt.

425
00:28:01,801 --> 00:28:07,800
Meanwhile, in a much cooler part of the foundry, a parallel process of mold making is underway.

426
00:28:07,801 --> 00:28:18,800
Using specifications set by Ping's design team, mold makers replicate every minute detail of each club in wax.

427
00:28:18,801 --> 00:28:22,040
It's cordoba beeswax.

428
00:28:22,041 --> 00:28:26,840
It's very critical in the process because in order to gain dimensional control, this

429
00:28:26,841 --> 00:28:29,520
wax has to be stable.

430
00:28:29,521 --> 00:28:33,920
The wax also has to be able to handle a heavy coating of ceramic.

431
00:28:33,921 --> 00:28:40,040
The next critical step in the process is to apply ceramic slurry to our wax pattern setup.

432
00:28:40,041 --> 00:28:44,280
We're going to load the setup on the robot hanger.

433
00:28:44,281 --> 00:28:47,180
The robot's gonna come over, pick up the setup,

434
00:28:47,181 --> 00:28:50,700
dip it in a water-based slurry, just to add a slip plane.

435
00:28:52,440 --> 00:28:57,440
So that our first coat of primary dip adheres to the product.

436
00:29:05,560 --> 00:29:08,440
From there, we're gonna go into a fluidized sand bed

437
00:29:08,441 --> 00:29:10,840
and add the ceramic sand

438
00:29:10,841 --> 00:29:13,360
so that we build up a layer of slurry sand.

439
00:29:14,280 --> 00:29:16,460
We're going to repeat that four more times.

440
00:29:16,860 --> 00:29:19,160
Four coats of slurry, four coats of sand.

441
00:29:20,380 --> 00:29:26,160
Each coat must be applied extremely evenly to capture every intricate line and groove of the wax mold.

442
00:29:26,900 --> 00:29:29,780
This process of dipping will take 22 hours.

443
00:29:30,460 --> 00:29:33,320
It's a job that's too delicate and labor-intensive for humans.

444
00:29:33,960 --> 00:29:39,640
We use robotics so that we get the same consistent result time in and time again.

445
00:29:40,480 --> 00:29:44,220
Once the ceramic shell is hardened, the wax is melted out.

446
00:29:44,280 --> 00:29:46,580
leaving a hollow mold ready for the poor.

447
00:29:48,780 --> 00:29:51,240
Handling molten metal is extremely dangerous.

448
00:29:52,520 --> 00:29:55,560
Pourers must wear protective suits made of aluminized Kevlar,

449
00:29:56,000 --> 00:29:58,220
the same material used for bulletproof vests.

450
00:29:59,460 --> 00:30:03,180
The Kevlar shields workers from accidental splashes of white-hot metal.

451
00:30:04,400 --> 00:30:08,160
But perhaps even more deadly is the intense radiant heat.

452
00:30:10,680 --> 00:30:13,480
Exposure to this invisible force can be fatal.

453
00:30:14,280 --> 00:30:19,060
Protective attire is not only critical, it's life-saving.

454
00:30:20,680 --> 00:30:24,640
Before pouring the metal, workers must remove a layer of slack,

455
00:30:25,100 --> 00:30:28,680
a thick crust of unwanted metals that floats to the top during smelting.

456
00:30:30,780 --> 00:30:32,160
And pouring commences.

457
00:30:56,683 --> 00:31:05,160
As the molds cool, the temperature of the molten steel plummets, going from 3,000 degrees Fahrenheit to room temperature in just four hours.

458
00:31:06,160 --> 00:31:10,780
But not even Arnold Palmer could play around with this, at least not yet.

459
00:31:10,781 --> 00:31:14,280
There's one more stop before we're ready to hit the green.

460
00:31:15,540 --> 00:31:18,040
And another stop for these auto bodies, too.

461
00:31:18,280 --> 00:31:21,560
Because once the paint is dry, it's time to fill them up.

462
00:31:22,180 --> 00:31:24,060
But what about that modular home?

463
00:31:24,380 --> 00:31:28,860
It took six hours to set the cubes, but it'll take less than a week to build them.

464
00:31:29,740 --> 00:31:31,860
It's start to finish in overdrive.

465
00:31:32,240 --> 00:31:34,180
Next, on Modern Marvels.

466
00:31:36,840 --> 00:31:40,100
Start to finish, building a B-2 stealth bomber.

467
00:31:40,780 --> 00:31:46,080
One of the most important steps is the application of a special paint that absorbs radar waves,

468
00:31:46,580 --> 00:31:48,560
allowing the plane to go undetected.

469
00:31:51,080 --> 00:31:54,140
It's been a successful day for Terrence Hegel and his crew.

470
00:31:55,520 --> 00:31:58,600
We built a 2,000-square-foot house in six hours.

471
00:31:59,240 --> 00:32:02,300
If we had built that house on site in a conventional method,

472
00:32:02,660 --> 00:32:05,880
where we're at today would have taken three months.

473
00:32:06,920 --> 00:32:09,740
Three months is an eternity in the modular world,

474
00:32:09,741 --> 00:32:14,240
where construction speed can outpace conventional building 25 to 1.

475
00:32:15,200 --> 00:32:18,140
But setting a home is actually the final step.

476
00:32:19,240 --> 00:32:21,780
Modular building begins in the factory.

477
00:32:22,560 --> 00:32:25,060
We've basically taken the house and put it in the factory

478
00:32:25,061 --> 00:32:29,080
just like Henry Ford put the Model T in the factory all those years ago.

479
00:32:29,540 --> 00:32:34,800
This is Xcel Builders, North America's largest modular home factory.

480
00:32:34,801 --> 00:32:40,800
Here, Henry Ford's assembly line system is on spectacular display

481
00:32:40,801 --> 00:32:45,200
As modules make their way from one end of the factory to the other

482
00:32:45,201 --> 00:32:48,860
It's an astonishingly efficient process

483
00:32:48,861 --> 00:32:52,600
We're going to go from a pile of lumber over on that side of the factory

484
00:32:52,601 --> 00:32:57,520
